```json leader-stories
[
{"story": "Best-of-N submissions of a design in our class. The live 0.7435 is +0.0076 over our 0.7359, inside the +-0.008 per-submission spread the ledger measured (identical code ran at speed factors 0.965-1.045 across r8-r10 of #35; our S rows move +-1.2 us between unchanged submissions). A team that resubmits its merge 3-5 times keeps its best draw; our r9-merge was submitted once.", "consistent_with": "Our merges land within 0.002 of projection on M/L rows but the S rows swing +-0.01 of S each; 7 S rows x +0.01 / 16 = +0.004 of mean from one lucky draw, and the tensor-heavy rows add a correlated 2-3%.", "test": "Resubmit r9-merge twice (2 of the 5 free daily slots) and record the spread; if the best of three exceeds 0.740 the gap is mostly noise and every real gain should also be submitted 2-3 times."},
{"story": "Same fused tcgen05 fp16 architecture, but the wide-C GEMMs run at the tensor ideal: cta_group::2 pairs at N >= 224, a weight-stationary GEMM2 with B-only stages, no g term, and a one-pass weight conversion off the critical path, so the six 7x7/14x14 rows sit at S ~0.74-0.76 (portal 36-70 us) while the S and top rows equal ours.", "consistent_with": "r9 E2's pair probe reaches 1.01x of the MMA ideal at N >= 224; r9 E1 shows phase A bound by the W1 stream and GEMM2 by 48 KB latency-bound stages; the ledger's own arithmetic says lifting those six rows to 0.74 is worth +0.017, more than the whole gap.", "test": "Idea 1's microbench (B-only 16 KB stages at depth 12 against the current 0.7 us per 48 KB stage) plus a balanced-tile GEMM1 stamp at 16,384; if the GEMM1 phase drops below 10 us and GEMM2 stages below 0.3 us the story is reproducible in one lineage round."},
{"story": "The leader wins the seven C <= 192 rows, not the wide-C rows: a single-launch kernel that never materialises the hidden (hidden tile kept on chip across one GRN barrier, GEMM2 straight from it) lands 1,192 / 1,96 / 2,96 / 4,192 / 8,192 / 1,160 / 4,96 at ~17-20 us portal where we sit at 23-55, which alone is worth +0.02-0.03 of mean even with wide-C rows 10% slower than ours.", "consistent_with": "Our H11 stub floor for a two-barrier coop kernel is 4.6 us rented and sc_kernel's remaining S-row time is 3 chunked GEMM1 tiles plus a materialised hidden (12-15 us at 4,192 / 8,192); lineage C's on-chip attempt failed on mma.sync skeleton costs, not on the idea, and TMEM gives the capacity C lacked.", "test": "Build idea 10 for 4,192 and 8,192 first (the two sc rows with the most GEMM time); if either lands below 25 / 32 us rented the story holds and the remaining sc rows follow in a second round."}
]
```

Recap: the leader-gap table is stale (it implies a 0.69 leader we already beat), so the stories target the live 0.7435. The ten ideas split into D-kernel refinements with concrete arithmetic (resident-A GEMM2 with balanced N=256 GEMM1 tiles, in-ring W1 conversion, TF32 zero-conversion with the MMA-floor caveat, 2 CTAs/SM, bulk reduce-add stores, cache hints) and four long shots (cluster-conv DSMEM all-gather, A operand from TMEM, k-stationary 2,768 specialist, on-chip TMEM hidden for C <= 192). Each has a 10-minute microbench gate; the resident-A GEMM2 idea is the one I'd start first, since it attacks the two largest post-sync terms on the six lowest-scoring rows at once.

